## Training

Fine-tuned from `lt_LT-reginute1-medium` (CC BY 4.0, trained on the LIEPA
corpus of Vilnius University), which is itself fine-tuned from the Piper
catalogue checkpoint `ru_RU-irina-medium`, fine-tuned in turn from
`en_US-lessac-medium` (see piper-voices discussion #94 on that base). Trained
with our own tools.

What is published here is the **voice**, not the means of making one: the
exported model, its config, the phonemizer and its dictionary. The training
checkpoint is deliberately not distributed.

## Phonemization — please read

This voice uses `phoneme_type: text` with a custom `phoneme_id_map` (IPA plus
three Lithuanian accent marks `ˈ ˌ ˋ`), the same map as Reginutė. Text must be
phonemized by `phonemize_lithuanian.py` with its three tables
(`lt_kirciai.tsv`, `lt_raides.tsv`, `lt_kreipiniai.tsv`). Plain Piper without
the phonemizer feeds the model raw letters and will not produce Lithuanian.

The phonemizer is merged into piper1-gpl and will ship with Piper 1.9.0; with
that Piper, a config saying `"phoneme_type": "lithuanian"` runs it
automatically. Until then, phonemize with the module and pass IPA to Piper —
`synth_reginute.py` (shared with Reginutė) does exactly that.

The module also normalizes text first (`skaiciu_pletiklis.py`, picked up when
present; `expand_text=None` disables it): number case endings, clock times,
units and letter-by-letter abbreviations.

Recommended: `normalize_audio=False`, and long text split at punctuation
(`ReginuteSynth` does both).

## Known limitations

* **The voice needs the phonemizer** until Piper 1.9.0 — see above.
* **The stress dictionary has individual errors.** It is derived, not checked
  word by word; words missing from it get espeak-ng's own guess.
* **Homographs are not solved** — `nãmo` and `namõ` are spelled the same and
  differ only in accent; a word-level lookup cannot choose.
* **`length_scale` 1.15 is a judgement by ear.** Override it freely.

